  "confidence": 0.72,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R4", "R5", "R6"],
      "pins": ["REG1.24"],
      "nets": ["REG_FB", "+5V"],
      "observed": "The MIC26903 feedback network on the Power sheet shows R5 = 6k8 from +5V to REG_FB, with R4 = 19k6 and R6 = 1k3 both from REG_FB to GND. The schematic note states Vout = 0.8*(1+R5/R6).",
      "expected": "For a 5 V output with the MIC26903 0.8 V reference, the divider should be R5/R6 = 5.25, i.e. R5 = 6k8 with R6 = 1k3 (giving ~4.98 V). R4 = 19k6 in parallel with R6 gives an effective bottom resistance of ~1.22k and Vout ~5.26 V, which is inconsistent with the stated 5 V rail and the note's own formula.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "5 V rail: A MIC26903YJL-TR synchronous buck regulator steps the battery input down to 5 V. Design notes on this section: ... feedback divider relation \"Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)\""},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet: R5 6k8, R4 19k6, R6 1k3, C7 4n7, note \"Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)\""}
      ],
      "impact": "The 5 V rail would regulate to approximately 5.26 V instead of 5 V, over-volting the 5 V servo/GPS/USB-UART loads and the 3.3 V LDO input.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design is largely consistent with the specification, but the MIC26903 5 V feedback network on the Power sheet appears to contain an extra/incorrect bottom resistor (R4 = 19k6 in parallel with R6 = 1k3), which would set the rail to about 5.26 V rather than the intended 5 V."
}

